## Phase 2: Code review & merge

1. Open a **pull request** to **`main`**.
2. Team review + automated checks (when configured).
3. Merge when approved. This triggers **Auto Version Bump** on **`main`** (version + native counters). **Android** and **iOS production EAS builds** run only when you **manually** start those workflows in **Actions**.

```bash
git push origin feature/your-feature-name
```

---

## Phase 3a: Android production release (manual GitHub Action)

**What it does:** Runs **`eas build --platform android --profile production`** from the current **`main`** checkout. Uses **`EXPO_TOKEN`** and the same EAS **`production`** profile as local CLI builds.

**When:** **Actions → Android Production Build → Run workflow** (branch **`main`**), after **Auto Version Bump** has completed so the tree matches the release numbers you intend to ship.

**Versioning:** The workflow **does not** change **`package.json`** or **`app.config.js`**. Rely on the latest **Auto Version Bump** commit on **`main`**, or bump manually before running if you have an exceptional case.

**Install / distribution:** Download the APK from the **EAS build** page for that run, or publish to **GitHub Releases** manually if you want a stable download link for testers (the workflow itself no longer auto-creates a GitHub Release).

---

## Phase 3a-play: Android Play bundle / AAB (manual GitHub Action)

**What it does:** Runs **`eas build --platform android --profile production-play`** from the current checkout. Same **`APP_VARIANT: production`** and **`com.sleepfactor.app`** as the APK profile; **`eas.json`** sets **`android.buildType`** to **`app-bundle`** for this profile only.

**When:** **Actions → Android Play Bundle (AAB) → Run workflow** (branch **`main`** recommended), after **Auto Version Bump** on **`main`** so **`versionCode`** matches what you intend to upload to Play.

**Versioning:** Same as Phase 3a — the workflow does not bump versions; rely on the latest **Auto Version Bump** commit.

**Distribution:** Download the **.aab** from the **EAS** build page, then in **Play Console** attach it to **Internal testing**, **Closed testing**, or **Production** as appropriate. Automated **`eas submit --platform android`** (with a Google Play service account secret) is not wired yet; it can mirror the iOS submit workflow when you are ready.

**Local equivalent:** `npx eas-cli build --platform android --profile production-play --non-interactive`

---

## Phase 3b: iOS production / TestFlight (EAS — manual steps)

Use this path for **TestFlight** and **App Store** binaries. Production iOS builds use **`eas.json`** profile **`production`**: **`distribution: store`**, **`APP_VARIANT: production`**, **`com.sleepfactor.app`**, **Release** configuration, **Xcode 26.2** EAS image (App Store Connect minimum SDK; configured in **`eas.json`**).

**GitHub path (preferred for CI parity):** **Actions → iOS TestFlight Release → Run workflow** on **`main`** after **Auto Version Bump**. Same **`EXPO_TOKEN`** plus App Store Connect API secrets as configured in the repo.

### One-time / occasional setup

- **Apple Developer Program** and **App Store Connect** app record for **`com.sleepfactor.app`**.
- **Expo account** with access to the project (**EAS**).
- **`eas login`** locally; first **`eas submit`** may create an **App Store Connect API key** stored for EAS (approve **Y** when prompted).
- **Encryption:** **`ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption`** is set in **`app.config.js`** for standard TLS-only apps to avoid interactive export-compliance prompts where possible.

### Every new iOS build you want on TestFlight

1. **Commit** the code you intend to ship (reproducible state).
2. **Versions on `main`:** ensure **Auto Version Bump** has run after your merge so **`package.json`** and **`ios.buildNumber`** (and Android **`versionCode`**) are ahead of the last store upload. Apple rejects duplicate **CFBundleVersion** — the auto job normally prevents drift.
3. **Production Supabase:** apply any new migrations to the **production** database before testers use the new binary (see **Staged checklist** above).
4. **Cloud build + submit (pick one):**  
   - **CI:** **Actions → iOS TestFlight Release → Run workflow** on **`main`**.  
   - **Local:** `eas build --platform ios --profile production` then `eas submit --platform ios --latest` (or `npx eas-cli …` if `eas` is not global; submit from Expo dashboard also works).
5. **Wait for processing** in App Store Connect (often ~5–15+ minutes). Email may notify when done.
6. **TestFlight:** Open the app → **TestFlight** tab (not only the “Prepare for submission” App Store row). Add the new build to **Internal** and **External** groups as needed.
7. **Testers:** Install/update via Apple’s **TestFlight** app (updates appear as **Update** for existing testers).

**Internal testers:** Apple IDs on your **App Store Connect** team (**Users and Access**). Usually no separate Beta App Review for internal.

**External testers:** Invited by email in an **External Testing** group; **first** external build often undergoes **Beta App Review**.

**“Prepare for submission” (1.0) on the Apps tab:** That is the **App Store listing** track for a future **public store** release — separate from TestFlight. TestFlight builds still appear under **TestFlight** once processed.

### iOS vs OTA (Expo Updates)

The project may use **`expo-updates`** with an EAS **channel** (**`production`** in **`eas.json`**). **JavaScript-only** changes can sometimes ship via **EAS Update** without a new store binary — **native** or **config plugin** changes require a **new `eas build`**. When in doubt, ship a **new production iOS build** for TestFlight.

### iOS native build issues

If **EAS iOS** fails (e.g. Sentry / Xcode / HealthKit), see **`Learnings.mdc`** (Sentry Android autolinking exclude, iOS **`SWIFT_ENABLE_EXPLICIT_MODULES`** Podfile workaround, Nitro pins, etc.).

